WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.783 --> 00:00:08.200
MATT BALDWIN: So
I'm Matt Baldwin.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.700
I'm the director of cloud native
engineering here at NetApp,

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.440
and I'm here today to talk to
you about NetApp's cloud data

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.360
services strategy,
and how we're trying

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.930
to simplify enterprise
multi-cloud deployments

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.990
of cloud native infrastructure
and cloud native workload.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.180
We build this on top of
six different pillars

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:28.790
that we've defined in
our product, which is--

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.630
the first is how do
we simplify enterprise

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:31.780
multi-cloud Kubernetes?

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:34.320
And what we mean by that is,
what we're trying to arrive at

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.570
is, that you do not have to
worry about the cloud provider

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.340
that you're building
your cluster on top of,

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.257
that you're managing
infrastructure on top of,

00:00:40.257 --> 00:00:43.060
that the experience
matches the same.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.030
So if you're using
Google, that experience

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.670
is the same as Amazon, as
is the same for on premise.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.920
And that's what we're trying
to do with our enterprise

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.110
multi-cloud Kubernetes offering.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:54.387
Our second pillar is
application management.

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:56.970
And we're trying to follow the
same theme there, which is to--

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.940
how do you simplify the
developer experience

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.880
with building, shipping, and
running applications inside

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:03.600
of Kubernetes?

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.030
How do you ensure those
applications can scale?

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:07.290
How do you automate that?

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.323
How do you scale them
across multiple providers?

00:01:09.323 --> 00:01:10.740
How do you scale
them from on-prem

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.755
to off prem, in a
hybrid scenario?

00:01:12.755 --> 00:01:14.880
And that is what we're
driving out with application

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.400
management-- essentially how
do you transform a Kubernetes

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.337
cluster into a Heroku-like
experience for the developer?

00:01:20.337 --> 00:01:22.170
So they never have to
understand Kubernetes.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:23.520
They don't need to
know what Istio is.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:24.690
They can perform a canary.

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.410
Not a problem.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:28.350
The third component
that we stand on top of

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.970
is manage data
services and storage.

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:34.440
So NetApp is a storage
company as many people know.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.570
And we have over 20
years of experience

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.120
in enterprise grade storage
and delivering solutions out

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.647
to consumers and to customers.

00:01:41.647 --> 00:01:43.230
And what we're doing
is, we're working

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:46.440
to lift all of those
features into, and pull those

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.770
into Kubernetes, so that we
can guarantee your application.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.100
We can protect it.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:53.220
We can guarantee
IOPS for your app.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:56.100
We can move your app around
from provider to provider

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:57.630
and the storage will follow you.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:00.240
Snapshot, mirror,
clone, all that goodness

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.070
comes from the NetApp
technology that we're

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:03.690
pulling up into Kubernetes.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.000
And that's one main, one
of the primary pillars

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.820
that we're standing on top
of, is that we were able to--

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.369
we've solved the stateful
workload problem in Kubernetes

00:02:11.369 --> 00:02:12.300
at NetApp.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.350
And that's very important, as
you guys probably all know,

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:17.520
for Kubernetes.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.460
The fourth pillar is
ecosystem integration.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.220
What we mean there is we don't
mean just taking Helm charts

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.532
and throwing them into a
bucket of a marketplace,

00:02:25.532 --> 00:02:27.240
and then letting the
user figure out what

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:28.352
they need to do with that.

00:02:28.352 --> 00:02:30.060
When we're talking
ecosystem integration,

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.400
we're talking about deeply
integrating with ecosystem

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.853
partners to deliver a value out
to their users, versus being

00:02:36.853 --> 00:02:39.270
a feature farm where you're
trying to just iterate through

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:40.478
as many features as possible.

00:02:40.478 --> 00:02:44.110
What we're trying to do
is arrive at user value.

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:46.830
So when that user clicks
up and slides that slider,

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.290
enables that feature, that
they immediately have value

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.305
that they can consume.

00:02:51.305 --> 00:02:52.680
And we have a
partner that we can

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.420
bring on stage here
in a bit, to talk

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.570
about that, that type of
ecosystem integration.

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:59.670
The fifth item is,
again, we're trying

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:02.920
to build simple tooling
for developers, operators,

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:03.420
and SREs.

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:08.820
As our SRE teams figure
out and solve issues,

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:12.710
we're pushing those
feature sets, those types

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:14.480
of dashboards, down
to the end users,

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:17.430
so those users can
use those things.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.280
The sixth pillar for
us is channel partners.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.352
So our product actually
enables you to rebrand.

00:03:25.352 --> 00:03:27.310
Anyone can launch their
own Kubernetes service.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:28.600
We can partner with anyone.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:30.600
We have a partner we're
bringing on stage again,

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.738
who will show you one
of the partnerships

00:03:32.738 --> 00:03:33.780
that we're doing on that.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.900
But it allows us to enable
our enterprise partners

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.127
to sell to their
enterprises, and enable that

00:03:39.127 --> 00:03:39.960
through the channel.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.010
So we not only go direct, we
also go through the channel.

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:43.590
And that's one of
the big pillars

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:47.050
that we stand on top of.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:48.838
So why are we here today?

00:03:48.838 --> 00:03:50.880
We have four things that
we're going to announce.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.470
First, that we're
working with NVIDIA.

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:54.750
NVIDIA and NetApp
are working together

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.550
to deliver an NVIDIA
Kubernetes Services

00:03:56.550 --> 00:04:01.240
Product that focuses on deep
learning, AI, ML, and I have--

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:04.890
Adel El Hallak will be coming
on stage to talk about that.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:08.520
The second item is our cloud
native application management

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:10.410
is going to go to
pre-release, and that's

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:12.077
going to be a bulk
of this presentation.

00:04:12.077 --> 00:04:14.298
We're going to show
you what that is.

00:04:14.298 --> 00:04:15.840
And I'm going to
have Dan Norris, one

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.215
of my senior
engineers, will come

00:04:17.215 --> 00:04:20.579
on stage to demo that, and
take you through all that work.

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:22.560
And again, our focus
has always been

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.227
to simplify the
experience for the user,

00:04:24.227 --> 00:04:26.102
and I think that's going
to come through when

00:04:26.102 --> 00:04:27.750
you start to see the
tooling that we've

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:30.450
built for developers.

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:32.672
The third item is,
we've enabled what

00:04:32.672 --> 00:04:34.380
we call an NKS enterprise
registry, which

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:35.790
is built on top of--

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:39.630
we've effectively integrated
into the CNC of Harbor Project,

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:42.940
to enable enterprise
registry in public cloud,

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:45.900
and private cloud as well, and
that's all wrapped up inside

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:48.060
of our workflow.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:51.083
The fourth item is cloud
volume services on GCP,

00:04:51.083 --> 00:04:53.250
and that we're going to be
going into beta with that

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:54.420
at the end of May.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.850
And because storage is rather
important to applications.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:59.100
And so we're going to
conclude today's presentation

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.750
with a discussion around cloud
native persistent storage,

00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:04.630
and how that relates
to what we're doing.

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:07.160
And I have Alim
coming on to do that.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:09.270
So first I want
to bring up Adel,

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:12.506
who will talk about our NVIDIA
partnership on Kubernetes.

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:19.490
ADEL EL HALLAK: Thanks, Matt.

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:20.490
Good morning, everyone.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:21.657
My name's is Adel El Hallak.

00:05:21.657 --> 00:05:24.120
I'm director of product
management over at NVIDIA.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.030
I looked after NGC.

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:27.980
So real quick, what is NGC?

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:30.860
NGC is an accelerated
software hub,

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.100
some of the most popular
accelerated applications

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.720
out there in the marketplace.

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:38.150
We start off with HPC
and visualization,

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:40.910
but actually that bleeds
over into ML and DL,

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:42.860
which is a huge focus of ours.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:45.380
So it started off as
a container registry,

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:47.750
but we've expanded a lot
more than that since.

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:50.480
We've since introduced
pre-trained models that

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:53.720
are domain specific,
across medical imaging,

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:55.352
or video analytics.

00:05:55.352 --> 00:05:56.810
Also some models
that are up there,

00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:58.700
just ready for inference.

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:00.650
We've also introduced
model training scripts,

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:03.987
so teaching entities how to
train to state of the art

00:06:03.987 --> 00:06:06.320
accuracy and get models how
to converge state of the art

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.050
accuracy from scratch, right?

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:09.890
Study exactly what
container to pull where,

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:12.687
where to get your data sets,
what learning rate the set,

00:06:12.687 --> 00:06:14.270
and be able to
replicate some of those

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:16.070
[? anchors ?] that we drive.

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:18.290
And again, growing
above and beyond that,

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:19.850
where we really
start deriving value

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:23.120
is when you become domain
specific, industry specific.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:24.650
So as Matt alluded
to earlier, we

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:26.720
are introducing industry
specific Helm charts,

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:31.040
or workflows, that frankly go
from an end to end perspective.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:33.230
But why are we here, right?

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:35.370
We realized that
that wasn't enough.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:37.950
And I love this theme
that Matt's been driving,

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:40.430
which is a simplification
theme, right?

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:43.400
This is a software hub.

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:44.870
It's almost giblets.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:49.070
The reality is data
science is a team sport.

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:51.530
And often we talk about
the data scientists.

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:53.000
We talk about the developers.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:54.980
But it takes an array
of different personas

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:56.550
collaborating
together, to realizing

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.070
the right value, right?

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:00.410
Wouldn't even talk
about DevOps, systems

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:03.500
admin, IT managers, who have
to worry about auto scaling

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:06.290
or have to worry
about OSs that are

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:09.600
supported in their data centers
or in their respective clouds.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.090
So how do we alleviate
some of those concerns?

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:19.010
And this is why we're partnering
with NetApp on the NKS service.

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:22.222
So real quick, let me
show you, if you don't

00:07:22.222 --> 00:07:23.430
mind going through computers.

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:25.310
Let me show you what
NGC is all about.

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:27.730
As I said earlier,
containers across an array

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:31.540
of different industries and
segments come to deep learning

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:32.800
here.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:35.140
Our engineers, our
data scientists,

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:38.500
optimize against our
GPO accelerations,

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:40.593
optimize TensorFlow.

00:07:40.593 --> 00:07:42.010
We upstream those
to the community

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:43.700
through the open source
community, as well.

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:45.670
But we also post those
in [? NGCS ?] containers

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:47.170
on a monthly basis.

00:07:47.170 --> 00:07:50.080
As I said earlier, we've
got pre-train models

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:52.180
across an array of couple
of different industries,

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:54.217
and some that are already
ready for inference,

00:07:54.217 --> 00:07:56.050
and these model scripts
that I talked about.

00:07:56.050 --> 00:08:01.480
Again, really showing you how
to reach a state of the art

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:03.580
accuracy, get your
models to converge,

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:05.560
and be able to build on that.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:10.630
But as I said earlier,
that's not enough, right?

00:08:10.630 --> 00:08:13.420
How do we go from use
case to actually being

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:15.065
able to deploy a prototype?

00:08:15.065 --> 00:08:16.690
And so what we're
introducing, and what

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:19.967
we're going to be showing later
on at the booth on the demo is,

00:08:19.967 --> 00:08:21.550
if you go back to
the slides, please--

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:28.490
is the ability to deploy a
container or a Helm chart

00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:32.090
directly through the NGC portal,
through the NetApp Kubernetes

00:08:32.090 --> 00:08:34.039
service.

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:35.953
So as a data scientist
or an ML, doesn't

00:08:35.953 --> 00:08:37.370
matter what your
proficiency level

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:40.039
is, to be able to
deploy with a few clicks

00:08:40.039 --> 00:08:43.970
onto your favorite cloud service
provider or on-prem seamlessly,

00:08:43.970 --> 00:08:46.200
with just a few clicks.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:48.317
And we're just starting
with inferencing.

00:08:48.317 --> 00:08:49.650
Inference is the first use case.

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:51.692
And that's the one we're
going to be demoing over

00:08:51.692 --> 00:08:53.120
at the booth at noon.

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:54.620
Again, with just a
few clicks, to be

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:56.780
able to deploy on
a Kubernetes based

00:08:56.780 --> 00:09:00.500
cluster, autoscale up and down
and as many nodes as possible,

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:05.300
our inference server,
which can concurrently

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:09.670
