00:11:08.102 --> 00:11:11.420
want as an SRE while actually
going through and delivering

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:13.382
value for your product.

00:11:13.382 --> 00:11:15.590
And we're not really standing
in the way here, right?

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:17.660
These are all sort
of opt in additions.

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:22.210
We're implementing them as
Kubernetes custom resources.

00:11:22.210 --> 00:11:25.160
So these are things that we are
pre-installing on your cluster,

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:30.890
ready to go, whenever you spin
up a Kubernetes cluster on NKS.

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:33.380
And so, things that
writing on here, too--

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:35.720
quick auto scaling,
resource management,

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:37.002
quota configuration.

00:11:37.002 --> 00:11:38.960
We've got a number of
different solution types,

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:41.335
and I'll walk through sort of
what that looks like, as we

00:11:41.335 --> 00:11:44.040
get a little bit into the demo.

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:46.280
So what you're going
to see sort of our take

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:49.830
on what we think application
management should look like.

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:53.810
So the way that we think
of it is that applications,

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:55.902
we deploy them into projects.

00:11:55.902 --> 00:11:57.860
If you're familiar with
Kubernetes terminology,

00:11:57.860 --> 00:11:59.930
a project is basically
an abstraction

00:11:59.930 --> 00:12:02.420
around Kubernetes namespaces.

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:04.980
We add a little bit
of magic on top of it,

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:07.530
again through using
custom resources.

00:12:07.530 --> 00:12:09.470
And then what we call
solutions are basically

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:12.020
definitions of how you
deploy your application.

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:15.030
It's not just a
deployment, a StatefulSet,

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:16.370
a daemon set, right?

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:17.450
This adds context.

00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:20.510
This adds value by
showing how you actually

00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:24.710
deliver your application,
how it gets to your cluster.

00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:27.278
So a little bit
more about projects.

00:12:27.278 --> 00:12:28.820
We think of it as
a container, right?

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:32.420
You want to take everything
that comprises your application.

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:35.990
So perfect example
is, say, WordPress.

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:37.550
You're going to
need MySQL server.

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:39.110
You're going to
need the WordPress

00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:42.140
PHP, and all the other
pages that go to that.

00:12:42.140 --> 00:12:44.270
That belongs all in
the same project.

00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:45.620
Those might be multiple pods.

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:47.870
You can scale them differently,
doesn't really matter,

00:12:47.870 --> 00:12:50.570
because it's still Kubernetes
at the end of the day.

00:12:50.570 --> 00:12:53.700
What we add on top of that is,
again, extensions to namespace.

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:56.990
So we hook into, through
our product, RBAC,

00:12:56.990 --> 00:12:59.780
so we effectively reflect
that into the clusters,

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:01.730
so that you as a
cluster admin can

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:03.650
take that and restrict
who has access

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:06.080
to these particular resources.

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:09.763
So say your SRE team is
allowed to make changes

00:13:09.763 --> 00:13:11.930
to your production applications,
but your developers

00:13:11.930 --> 00:13:13.760
are only allowed to read them.

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:16.935
That's what we've kind of
added as on an extension there.

00:13:16.935 --> 00:13:19.310
And so in terms of the solutions
that we've come up with,

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:23.000
we kind of targeted three
for our pre-release.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:24.838
So we have what we
call the tracker.

00:13:24.838 --> 00:13:27.380
And the tracker is kind of like
the lowest common denominator

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:28.640
of our solution.

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:31.430
It's basically-- you are
an experienced Kubernetes

00:13:31.430 --> 00:13:31.930
developer.

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:34.550
You're experienced at actually
delivering applications

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:36.020
and help the platform.

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:37.880
This is how you
effectively inform

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:40.410
our backend of what that is.

00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:44.210
So we can effectively adopt
your application, as is,

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:47.210
to keep your deploy
strategy, and it just works.

00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:49.460
Again, if you're familiar
with Kubernetes terminology,

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:52.020
this is effectively
just label selectors.

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:58.640
We use that to adopt an
existing StatefulSet,

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:01.630
and that way you can manage
it through our interface.

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:04.130
And then the next solution that
we have is the Git workflow.

00:14:04.130 --> 00:14:06.740
So effectively Git,
push, NKS master.

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:10.460
We want to be able to take
pretty much any application

00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:12.500
that you have in any
programming language,

00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:14.090
and make it that
simple to deploy,

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:17.520
and get a Kubernetes
deployment out of it.

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:20.340
And the third that we've
got our Helm charts, right?

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:23.270
CNCF going pretty
hard on actually

00:14:23.270 --> 00:14:26.185
having Helm charts be sort of
the package management solution

00:14:26.185 --> 00:14:26.810
for Kubernetes.

00:14:26.810 --> 00:14:28.590
So we want to support that.

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:31.558
So all we really need know is
where does your chart live,

00:14:31.558 --> 00:14:34.100
and what changes do you want to
make to its values.yaml which

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:35.332
is how you configure it.

00:14:35.332 --> 00:14:37.040
And we effectively
take care of the rest.

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:39.050
We deploy it, we
manage the lifecycle,

00:14:39.050 --> 00:14:40.595
and we do this actually--

00:14:40.595 --> 00:14:42.720
again, if you're kind of
familiar with the way that

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:46.070
Helm kind of works, it's a
client server application.

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:48.140
So we do it without
the server component.

00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:51.020
Because we've written custom
code and extensions on top

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:52.588
of your Kubernetes
cluster, we're

00:14:52.588 --> 00:14:54.380
able to actually lock
that down, giving you

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:58.620
a much more secure way to
actually deliver Helm charts.

