00:27:59.160 --> 00:28:00.660
region, and there's
a set of regions

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:02.310
that we're currently
available in.

00:28:02.310 --> 00:28:04.710
But the next big thing
is around performance.

00:28:04.710 --> 00:28:07.560
Cloud Volumes enables
a set of workloads

00:28:07.560 --> 00:28:09.570
now to run Google
Cloud, that was

00:28:09.570 --> 00:28:12.030
previously unable to run, ever.

00:28:12.030 --> 00:28:15.360
So we're able to provide
hundreds of thousands of IOPS,

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:19.170
and guarantee those via SLOs
at very, very low latency.

00:28:19.170 --> 00:28:21.990
So most of our regions
we deployed a service in,

00:28:21.990 --> 00:28:25.540
sub two milliseconds, you can
get for over 100,000 IOPS.

00:28:25.540 --> 00:28:27.630
So again, this opens
up a whole class

00:28:27.630 --> 00:28:29.310
of applications that
were previously not

00:28:29.310 --> 00:28:33.030
able to run on GCP.

00:28:33.030 --> 00:28:36.120
Finally, I mentioned this notion
of being built in partnership

00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:39.690
with Google, and being basically
a native service within GCP.

00:28:39.690 --> 00:28:41.050
What does this mean?

00:28:41.050 --> 00:28:43.140
It means that customers
are able to log in

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:46.740
through their familiar terms
with the cloud console,

00:28:46.740 --> 00:28:50.340
go into the cloud console
without any other identity

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:52.660
or access management
integration from NetApp-- just

00:28:52.660 --> 00:28:55.170
use your standard project
and the credentials

00:28:55.170 --> 00:28:57.360
that you use to log
into your project,

00:28:57.360 --> 00:29:00.750
discover the Cloud
Volumes UI, or the API,

00:29:00.750 --> 00:29:03.780
enable it, and go and
provision your volumes.

00:29:03.780 --> 00:29:06.610
That means that there is really
no leaving the Cloud console,

00:29:06.610 --> 00:29:08.910
so the whole experience
is very, very seamless

00:29:08.910 --> 00:29:11.560
like any other Google
native service.

00:29:11.560 --> 00:29:13.770
We also integrate with
Google Service Control

00:29:13.770 --> 00:29:15.990
for, those of you that
know that facility.

00:29:15.990 --> 00:29:20.040
It allows us to push all of our
metrics, logs and monitoring

00:29:20.040 --> 00:29:22.120
data into Google
Service Control.

00:29:22.120 --> 00:29:25.740
And so you can consume
that data from Stackdriver,

00:29:25.740 --> 00:29:28.770
and essentially monitor what
your file systems are doing,

00:29:28.770 --> 00:29:31.020
in terms of performance
availability,

00:29:31.020 --> 00:29:33.180
and then be able to even
monitor and set alerts

00:29:33.180 --> 00:29:35.190
from Stackdriver natively,
like you would do

00:29:35.190 --> 00:29:37.240
for any other Google service.

00:29:37.240 --> 00:29:39.120
And then finally, from
a support perspective,

00:29:39.120 --> 00:29:42.160
where this is aimed at
enterprise workloads,

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:45.000
so support is actually
provided directly by Google.

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:47.550
Google may escalate some issues
to NetApp in the backend,

00:29:47.550 --> 00:29:50.010
but by and large, you know
you as a Google customer

00:29:50.010 --> 00:29:55.320
deal directly with Google even
though it's NetApp service.

00:29:55.320 --> 00:29:57.598
So I could go on and on
