00:30:14.293 --> 00:30:15.210
LOUIS VERZI: Hi, Alim.

00:30:15.210 --> 00:30:16.620
Hello, everyone.

00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:18.780
Just wanted to talk
a little bit about,

00:30:18.780 --> 00:30:21.780
like Alim said,
how we came about,

00:30:21.780 --> 00:30:24.420
our decision to use NetApp
Cloud Volume Service,

00:30:24.420 --> 00:30:26.760
and how our experience
has been so far.

00:30:26.760 --> 00:30:32.280
So we have been spending
the last couple of years

00:30:32.280 --> 00:30:34.290
doing a lift and
shift migration,

00:30:34.290 --> 00:30:36.540
bringing existing applications
out of our data center,

00:30:36.540 --> 00:30:38.830
and into the cloud.

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:41.760
We've started with our
non-prod workloads, dev, QA,

00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:44.500
and have since moved on
to stage and prod systems.

00:30:44.500 --> 00:30:48.300
And about a year ago,
we switched over to GCP

00:30:48.300 --> 00:30:50.760
for many technical
and business reasons.

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:52.620
We really found GCP
was a better cloud

00:30:52.620 --> 00:30:55.230
platform for what we needed
to provide to our application

00:30:55.230 --> 00:30:57.090
teams.

00:30:57.090 --> 00:31:00.450
But as we started moving these
stage and production systems,

00:31:00.450 --> 00:31:05.750
we realized that we weren't
able to, as we wanted to,

00:31:05.750 --> 00:31:08.920
kind of get away from
network-attached storage.

00:31:08.920 --> 00:31:11.187
A lot of the applications
that we had out there--

00:31:11.187 --> 00:31:12.645
some of them are
COTS, some of them

00:31:12.645 --> 00:31:14.520
are homegrown
legacy applications.

00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:18.990
They're built on assuming that
they had NAS for providing

00:31:18.990 --> 00:31:21.010
high available solutions.

00:31:21.010 --> 00:31:24.030
And so we started looking
around for a solution that

00:31:24.030 --> 00:31:28.170
would give us the ability to
provision quickly on demand,

00:31:28.170 --> 00:31:31.380
not have to sign a PO
with another vendor,

00:31:31.380 --> 00:31:33.600
make commitments.

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:36.570
And NetApp met all
those things for us,

00:31:36.570 --> 00:31:42.120
so it's been really easy
to provision that storage

00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:45.297
and get data out to
our application teams.

00:31:45.297 --> 00:31:46.380
ALIM KARIM: Thanks, Louis.

00:31:46.380 --> 00:31:48.360
So could you maybe give
us a few more examples

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:51.330
of the types of applications
that you're running,

00:31:51.330 --> 00:31:56.080
as well as sort of what the
ramp journey looked like?

00:31:56.080 --> 00:31:56.830
LOUIS VERZI: Sure.

00:31:56.830 --> 00:32:03.150
We started out obviously
with lower environments.

00:32:03.150 --> 00:32:10.480
Our SAP platform
required a place for them

00:32:10.480 --> 00:32:15.015
to do their deployments,
do the environments.

00:32:15.015 --> 00:32:15.890
We started with that.

00:32:15.890 --> 00:32:19.030
We started with some other
low risk applications.

00:32:19.030 --> 00:32:23.350
We've since moved on
to production SAP.

00:32:23.350 --> 00:32:26.740
We've got our enterprise
content management

00:32:26.740 --> 00:32:29.380
that's going to be going into
Cloud Volumes in production

00:32:29.380 --> 00:32:30.000
here shortly.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:32.620
It's been in our performance
environment for a little while,

00:32:32.620 --> 00:32:36.400
so we're going to have 20-plus
terabytes of our enterprise

00:32:36.400 --> 00:32:40.240
content serving up through
our e-commerce site,

00:32:40.240 --> 00:32:42.520
and through our SAP
environment that is going

00:32:42.520 --> 00:32:45.490
to be running off of NetApp.

00:32:45.490 --> 00:32:48.953
And what was the last part?

00:32:48.953 --> 00:32:50.870
ALIM KARIM: The other
thing I was going to say

00:32:50.870 --> 00:32:53.640
is sort of, what has
that journey been like,

00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:55.785
in terms of using the service?

00:32:55.785 --> 00:32:58.200
LOUIS VERZI: The journey
has been very easy.

00:32:58.200 --> 00:33:01.860
So the upfront work is just
getting the networking set up,

00:33:01.860 --> 00:33:03.360
which with the beta
release, I think

00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:06.900
that's going to be even easier.

00:33:06.900 --> 00:33:10.680
But really the hardest part was
spending 30 minutes on a call

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:14.490
with the NetApp engineers to
get our networking connected up.

00:33:14.490 --> 00:33:19.200
After that, it literally is
seconds to set up a volume.

00:33:19.200 --> 00:33:22.380
We always start with
their standard tier,

00:33:22.380 --> 00:33:24.960
and if application teams
aren't getting the performance

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:26.700
that they need with
a click of a button,

00:33:26.700 --> 00:33:28.470
they can have twice the IOPS.

00:33:28.470 --> 00:33:29.970
With another click
of a button, they

00:33:29.970 --> 00:33:32.340
can have four times the IOPS.

00:33:32.340 --> 00:33:36.320
If they need to grow from two
terabytes to five terabytes,

00:33:36.320 --> 00:33:37.800
it's another click of a button.

00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:42.390
No restarts, no copying of
storage, no expanding arrays.

00:33:42.390 --> 00:33:45.482
None of that complex work that
system administrators have

00:33:45.482 --> 00:33:46.690
had to deal with in the past.

00:33:46.690 --> 00:33:50.190
So it's really a
very easy solution

00:33:50.190 --> 00:33:53.858
that we can put in the hands
of our application teams.

00:33:53.858 --> 00:33:56.150
ALIM KARIM: So one of the
things in terms of outcomes--

00:33:56.150 --> 00:33:58.880
when you take these applications
and move them to cloud,

00:33:58.880 --> 00:34:02.090
what kind of a time
based acceleration impact

00:34:02.090 --> 00:34:04.010
do you think having
this service has made,

00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:06.170
in terms of executing
those projects?

00:34:06.170 --> 00:34:06.920
LOUIS VERZI: Sure.

00:34:06.920 --> 00:34:09.949
If we had to refactor
these applications out

00:34:09.949 --> 00:34:12.530
of using network
attached storage,

00:34:12.530 --> 00:34:17.790
it would've taken months
or years, or longer.

00:34:17.790 --> 00:34:20.219
Really, it would have taken
months per application

00:34:20.219 --> 00:34:21.563
to get these refactored.

00:34:21.563 --> 00:34:23.730
At Cardinal, we've got
hundreds of applications that

00:34:23.730 --> 00:34:25.860
are relying on NAS storage.

00:34:25.860 --> 00:34:29.280
So it really saved
us years of effort

00:34:29.280 --> 00:34:31.392
of having to refactor
these applications,

00:34:31.392 --> 00:34:33.600
and it's something that
application teams can kind of

00:34:33.600 --> 00:34:35.489
do on their own
time now, where they

00:34:35.489 --> 00:34:41.270
can work on getting their
storage into other formats,

00:34:41.270 --> 00:34:45.156
that typically in a data center
you don't have access to.

00:34:45.156 --> 00:34:46.739
ALIM KARIM: So you
already pointed out

00:34:46.739 --> 00:34:48.449
one of the things
that you don't like,

00:34:48.449 --> 00:34:50.880
which was that the networking
stuff that we had to do.

00:34:50.880 --> 00:34:53.429
And with cloud it's
amazing how much networking

00:34:53.429 --> 00:34:54.389
stuff you have to do.

00:34:54.389 --> 00:34:56.040
But what are some
of the other things

00:34:56.040 --> 00:34:58.470
that you're basically looking
for enhancements around,

00:34:58.470 --> 00:35:00.930
that we're releasing in
the upcoming release?

00:35:00.930 --> 00:35:01.680
LOUIS VERZI: Sure.

00:35:01.680 --> 00:35:06.750
From being in a heavily
compliance regulated

00:35:06.750 --> 00:35:10.350
marketplace or industry,
a lot of our workloads

00:35:10.350 --> 00:35:14.430
are going to need
NFSv4 with encryption.

00:35:14.430 --> 00:35:16.710
Obviously you don't want
to have PHI data anywhere

00:35:16.710 --> 00:35:18.630
that's unencrypted.

00:35:18.630 --> 00:35:20.530
They're already doing
the encryption at rest.

00:35:20.530 --> 00:35:23.280
But we're looking forward to
being able to do encryption

00:35:23.280 --> 00:35:25.950
in transit, as well.

00:35:25.950 --> 00:35:30.540
The other thing is, DR, that
we don't have to think about.

00:35:30.540 --> 00:35:33.420
Right now, we're
using some services

00:35:33.420 --> 00:35:35.330
to sync our data
to another region.

00:35:35.330 --> 00:35:38.160
It would be great if you could
just, with a click of a button,

00:35:38.160 --> 00:35:39.850
have that replication
taken care of,

00:35:39.850 --> 00:35:42.790
and then we wouldn't
need to administer that.

00:35:42.790 --> 00:35:46.710
And the third thing
is access across VPCs.

00:35:46.710 --> 00:35:51.660
So right now, with the
networking setup that we have,

00:35:51.660 --> 00:35:56.340
we aren't able to access
data across our VPCs.

00:35:56.340 --> 00:35:59.040
But there should be some
solutions coming forward

00:35:59.040 --> 00:36:01.077
that'll solve that
problem for us.

00:36:01.077 --> 00:36:02.910
ALIM KARIM: Well thank
you very much, Louis.

