Many of the things
that Louis mentioned

00:36:14.890 --> 00:36:17.410
has been feedback that we've
gotten from Cardinal Health, as

00:36:17.410 --> 00:36:18.500
well as other customers.

00:36:18.500 --> 00:36:20.410
So with the beta
release that we're

00:36:20.410 --> 00:36:22.570
planning for the end of
May, essentially we're

00:36:22.570 --> 00:36:24.910
going to automate the whole
networking flow that Louis

00:36:24.910 --> 00:36:29.320
talked about, give users a lot
more control in terms of which

00:36:29.320 --> 00:36:33.010
VPCs, which shared VPCs
have access to the service,

00:36:33.010 --> 00:36:35.620
and how they govern permissions
within their organizations

00:36:35.620 --> 00:36:37.570
and their service
projects to get access

00:36:37.570 --> 00:36:38.770
to these data services.

00:36:38.770 --> 00:36:41.583
So if you've got service
projects, GKE clusters, that

00:36:41.583 --> 00:36:43.000
are in those service
projects, you

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:45.640
can actually have much
more granular connectivity

00:36:45.640 --> 00:36:47.500
into these data services.

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:49.450
We're also going
to be rolling out

00:36:49.450 --> 00:36:52.090
Trident, which is an
existing plugin that we have

00:36:52.090 --> 00:36:54.610
that will automate the
creation of persistent volumes

00:36:54.610 --> 00:36:56.710
and precision volume
claims when you're

00:36:56.710 --> 00:36:58.022
running stateful applications.

00:36:58.022 --> 00:36:59.980
That will automatically
provision Cloud Volumes

00:36:59.980 --> 00:37:02.770
in the backend, and connect
those to your Kubernetes

00:37:02.770 --> 00:37:03.580
environments.

00:37:03.580 --> 00:37:05.913
And obviously we're going to
be adding more regions here

00:37:05.913 --> 00:37:07.900
in the US, in Europe as well.

